    "title": "A herder saves his sheep and cattle during a storm by praying to the Virgin Mary",
    "title_amharic": "ወደ ድንግል ማርያም በመጸለይ በጎቹንና ከብቶቹን ከከባድ ዝናብና ነፋስ ያዳነው እረኛ",
    "incipit": "ወሀሎ፡ ፩ብእሲ፡ ኖላዊ፡ ዘይሬኢ፡ አልህምተ፡ ወእንስሳ፡ ወኮነ፡ ብእሲ፡ የዋህ፡ ዘያፈቅራ፡ ለእግዝእትነ፡ ማርያም፡ ድንግል፡ በክልኤ፡ በልቡ፡ ወኮነ፡ ይትለአክ፡ ወይቀውም፡ ቅድሜሃ፡ ውስተ፡ ኵሉ፡ መካን፡ ወኢያጸርዕ፡ ሰላመ፡ መልአክ፡ ዛቲ፡ ወኮነ፡ ዝንቱ፡ ሰላም፡ ውስተ፡ አፉሁ፡ መዐልተ፡ ወሌሊተ፡ በሑረቱ፡ ወበግብአቱ፡ በንብረቱ፡ ወበተንሥኦቱ። ወአመ፡ በዓላ፡ ለእግዝእትነ፡ ማርያም፡ ድንግል፡ በክልኤ፡ ተንሥአ፡ አውሎ፡ ወበረድ፡ በይእቲ፡ ሌሊት፡ ወሐለየ፡ ውእቱ፡ ብእሲ፡ ከመ፡ ይመውቱ፡ እንስሳሁ፡ በይእቲ፡ ሌሊት፡",
    "manuscript_count": 194,
    "most_read": true,
    "translations": [
      {
        "author": "Wallis Budge",
        "text": "<p>There was a certain herdsman who pastured sheep and cattle. He was a simple man who loved Our Lady Mary with all his heart. He served her and stood before her in every place, and he never ceased to say the salutation of the angel Gabriel to her; indeed, that salutation was in his mouth day and night, coming or going, in sitting and rising.</p>\n\n<p>Now a heavy mist rose on the night of the festival of Our Lady Mary, and it began to snow.</p>\n\n<p>The man expected that his beasts would die of the great cold, and he was sad. He went outside his house to look at the sky, and he began to pray to God.</p>\n\n<p>Then, turning his eyes to Our Lady Mary. He said, “My dear Lady Mary, be not far from my beasts, and have mercy upon your servants, who serve you day and night. Do not deprive these beasts of fodder; it is through the compassion of your son that they feed on your gracious gifts.”</p>\n\n<p>When he had finished his prayer, he fell to the ground. There was a break in the storm and a period of rest, and when the man saw [this], he gave thanks to Our Lady Mary, the mother of mercy.</p>\n\n<p>She appeared to him and said, “How long have you toiled? I wish to take you so that you may have rest.”</p>\n\n<p>He said, “Until when, my dear Lady?”</p>\n\n<p>She said, “Until the seventh day. Go to the monastery and tell the abbot that he must allow you to rest from your toil for seven days, by the word of the miracle that I spoke to him under the tree.”</p>\n\n<p>Then she disappeared.</p>\n\n<p>The shepherd went to the monastery, and when the monks saw him, they said, “Why have you come? Have you left your beasts?”</p>\n\n<p>He said, “I wish to speak to the abbot.”</p>\n\n<p>They said, “What can you want with the abbot? If you wish to obtain consolation [i.e., to confess], then speak to one of the brothers.”</p>\n\n<p>The man said, “I will speak to the abbot only.”</p>\n\n<p>The monks went to the abbot and told him about the shepherd’s arrival, and how he would speak to no one but him. They said, “Behold, he has come and left his beasts. We said to him, ‘If you wish to obtain consolation, then speak to one of the brothers.’ But he said, ‘I will only speak to the abbot.’”</p>\n\n<p>The abbot rose from his chamber and went to the shepherd.</p>\n\n<p>The shepherd bowed down at the abbot’s feet and told him all that Our Lady Mary had said to him, including the miracle [or sign] under the tree.</p>\n\n<p>When the abbot heard of this miracle, he understood and straightaway fell at the shepherd’s feet.</p>\n\n<p>The abbot took him to his own abode, dressed him in purple, he paid him honor and ministered to him, and asked him to intercede with God on his behalf.</p>\n\n<p>When seven days had passed, the abbot ordered the monks to leave their work and to gather in the chamber of the brothers. He then commanded the shepherd to pray on behalf of them all.</p>\n\n<p>The brothers marveled at that act, and they said to one another, “This man has no [ascetic] toil [to his credit]—what does he wish to do?”</p>\n\n<p>While the brothers were talking together, the shepherd died.</p>\n\n<p>The abbot began to tell the brothers the matter of the man and why Our Lady Mary had sent him.</p>\n\n<p>Then he urged them to increase their service to her, and to pay her special honor, for all those who minister to her she rewards with good things both in heaven and on earth.</p>\n\n<p>When the monks heard these words, they were exceedingly sorry that they had insulted the shepherd.</p>\n\n<p>They entreated God to forgive them their sins, and they gave thanks to Our Lady Mary, the compassionate.</p>"
      }
    ]
