    "title_amharic": "መጽሓፈ ራእየ ቴዎፍሎስ ዘእስክንድርያ፤ ክፍል 1.2፤ ድንግል ማርያም ሰይጣን ንጉሥ ሄሮድስ እንዴት እንደተገለጠለትና ንጹሓን ሕጻናትን እንዲገድል እንደመከረው ለእስክንድርያው ፓትርያርክ ቴዎፍሎስ ተረከችለት።",
    "incipit": "ወበይዕቲ፡ ዕለት፡ ሞተ፡ ስምዖን፡ ካህን፡ አረጋዊ፡ ወነበረ፡ ሄሮድስ፡ ፪ተ፡ ዓመተ፡ እንዘ፡ ይጸንሖሙ፡ ለሰብአ፡ ሰገል፡ ከመ፡ ይምጽአ፡ ኀቤሁ።",
    "manuscript_count": 3,
    "most_read": true,
    "translations": [
      {
        "author": "Wallis Budge",
        "text": "<p>[Our Lady Mary continues relating her story to Theophilus:]</p>\n\n<p>“Herod waited two years for the Magi to return to him, but they didn’t come back, for they had returned to their native land. Herod was greatly moved, and he and all his servants were afraid.</p>\n\n<p>“Then Satan appeared to him by night in the form of a wise man, and said, ‘Why do you remain [so idle]? For you and all your kingdom shall be destroyed.’</p>\n\n<p>“Herod answered, ‘What shall I do?’</p>\n\n<p>“Then Satan said, ‘Tomorrow, early in the morning, send your servants and command them not to leave [alive] in Bethlehem any child which is two years old or under. Let them slay them! And as the child [Jesus] shall be among those slain, he shall neither grow up nor take your kingdom.’</p>\n\n<p>“Now on that day, the angel of God appeared to Joseph and said, ‘Rise up, take the child and his mother, and depart to the country of Egypt until I give you his word, for Herod seeks to slay the child.’</p>\n\n<p>“Then straightaway we rose and departed to the country of Egypt. Salome was with us.</p>\n\n<p>“It came to pass that, when the next day had come, Herod commanded [his servants] to slay the children in all the districts of Bethlehem, in accordance with the information he had obtained from the Magi, and according to the word of Satan, the father of all iniquity.</p>\n\n<p>“So we rose up [and went] on our way. Joseph was in front of me. I carried my child sometimes on my shoulders, sometimes on my back, and sometimes in my arms. Because of the length of the way, and the weariness of the journey, Salome [carried him] in turn.</p>\n\n<p>“Sometimes I set him upon the ground so that he might follow me, as women do when they teach their children to walk. For I was a delicate woman, and it had not been my lot to toil hard. He would walk along a little way at a time, holding on to the hem of my skirt. Then, like all children who cry out to their mothers to carry them, he expected me to carry him, and I did so immediately on my back.</p>\n\n<p>“Then I would embrace him and rejoice in his walking. At the same time I would curse Herod and all his kingdom because he had treated innocent and sinless children in such a cruel manner. He had slain them and put sorrow into the breasts of their mothers and fathers.</p>\n\n<p>“Now, when Joseph saw Salome carrying the child upon her shoulders, he would carry our food for the way.</p>\n\n<p>“It came to pass that, when we arrived in the country of Egypt, we sat down outside a city, under the shadow of a tree, so that we might rest from the heat of the sun. The season was summer; the day was the twenty-seventh of Genbot [ca. May 22]. Joseph and Salome slept by reason of the weariness of the way, but I suckled my child.</p>\n\n<p>“[My Son’s] apparel was the color of wine. It was the same as my own, in which my father and my mother had dressed me when they brought me into the house of the sanctuary. Since the time when I first put it on, I had never taken it off. The sandals that were on my Son’s feet were like gold.</p>\n\n<p>“Behold, on that road [we met] two thieves. One of them belonged to the country of Egypt, and the other belonged to the country of the Hebrews.</p>\n\n<p>“The Hebrew said to the Egyptian, ‘I wish to steal the clothing from this woman and her child, for it looks like royal dress. If we had met them in the desert, I would have taken it, but they are close to a city.’</p>\n\n<p>“Then the Egyptian said to his companion, ‘Leave them alone and let us depart, for I have not seen a child like this since I was born.’</p>\n\n<p>“While they were talking in this way, I took out my breast and suckled the child, for I wished to give him milk to drink. When I looked about, I didn’t see them.</p>\n\n<p>“Then I rose and took the child into the city. I asked the women there to give him some water to drink, but none gave any, for the people of that city were merciless.</p>\n\n<p>“When the thieves saw that I had gone away, they came back and, going into the place where Joseph was sleeping, they carried off my Son’s shoes.</p>\n\n<p>“When I returned, I woke Joseph and Salome and said, ‘Get up, and let us depart from this city, for the people there have no mercy. I asked for water, but no one would give me any. I have lost nothing except my Son’s sandals, which the thieves have taken.’</p>\n\n<p>“As I said these words, I wept.</p>\n\n<p>“When my beloved Son saw my weeping, he motioned with his hand [to stop] my tears. He thrust his fingers down into the ground a little way, and immediately a pool of beautiful water welled up.</p>\n\n<p>“We drank from the well. The water was sweet as honey and white as milk.</p>\n\n<p>“Then he made the sign of the cross over the water and said, ‘Let this water be healing and pleasant to everyone who wants to drink from it, except to the people of this city. To them, let it be as gall in their mouths, and let not any one of them benefit from it.’</p>\n\n<p>“When we had eaten a little food, I took my beloved child upon my back, and we rose to depart.</p>\n\n<p>“Straightaway their temples fell, in the twinkling of an eye, and not one of them was left [standing]. Their idols were dashed to pieces, and their gods were thrown down, one upon the other, and broken.”</p>"
      }
    ]
